/* ---- Phase 34B: Two Lights, One Window, developed — Aquinas's "Three Lit Candles" ----
   Idea check: playing this, you feel two different lights, each following its own path, arrive at the same altar and light the same window.
   That is Aquinas's idea that faith and reason come from one source and can't truly contradict each other.
   Stages: I · Two Paths (clear glass: each beam must pass a pane and reach the altar) → II · Through the Glass (two puzzles; panes are colored and a beam takes the color of the last pane
   it passed; the altar lights only with white, which is amber+blue or rose+green) → III · The Rose Window (a close-up: turn three rings until both beams pass every ring and meet at the heart).
   Grid (Stages I-II, never drawn): 7 columns x 8 rows of 22-unit cells, origin (20,73). The gold beam enters at the top of column 3 heading down, the silver at the left of row 4 heading right;
   the altar is column 3, row 7. Mirrors have two orientations; a tap (or A on the pad's cursor) turns one: that is one turn. Beams pass through each other freely, and through panes.
   Rings (Stage III): rings 0-2 = ring1 (inner) .. ring3 (outer) of the art, 16 positions of 22.5 degrees. s.rot[k] is the step the ring stands at; OPEN[k] lists where its openings sit on the ring.
   The gold beam comes from 12 o'clock (position 0), the silver from 9 o'clock (position 12). A ring stops a beam unless an opening lies across its line.
   State: s.st stage, s.pz puzzle 0-2, s.mir[{c,r,o,f}] mirrors (o "/" or "\\", f drawn frame), s.turns (every mirror tap and every ring step), s.minTurns (fewest possible over all puzzles),
   s.candles lit, s.rot[3], s.ra[3] drawn angle, s.sel the ring the keys turn, s.ph play|lit (Stage III), s.fade 0..1 the crossfade into the close-up.
   Controls: tap a mirror; or pad cursor + A. Stage III: drag a ring around (the touch's angle about the centre turns the ring it started on), or left/right picks a ring and up/down (A / B) turns it. */
